Found while fixing k8 (reproduced by the worker): apply_changes writes with spew_raw, which creates a fresh file, so a chmod 755 script comes out 0664 (umask). Keep the mode of the old file on the new one, for a modified file and for a renamed+modified one. Test with an executable file in a temp tree.

2026-10-21 20:35 t/mode.t written first: 3 asserts red on main (0755 -> 0664 in place and renamed+modified; a 0600 file came out 0664, i.e. world-readable). Fix in lib/App/codefork.pm: apply writes through _write_file - File::Temp file in the target dir (created 0600), content appended, chmod to the old file's mode & 07777, then one rename onto the new path. So there is no window in which a private file is readable, and the replace stays atomic. Pure renames keep their mode as before (pinned). Asserts no .codefork-* temp file is left. apply_changes POD gets one sentence. Green.

